2. Driver architecture — GOOD

We have:

Test
 ↓
BaseTest
 ↓
DriverFactory
 ↓
DriverManager
 ↓
ThreadLocal<WebDriver>

And DriverFactory supports:

Local execution
Docker execution
LambdaTest
Chrome
Firefox
Edge
Headless execution
Insecure certificates
Download configuration
Driver timeouts
Configuration-driven execution

This is particularly good because you can explain:

"I don't instantiate WebDriver directly inside test classes. Driver creation is centralized in DriverFactory, while DriverManager controls the lifecycle and provides thread-safe driver access."

That's a senior-level answer.

Your actual experience also supports this architecture: your resume says you have worked with Selenium 4, parallel execution frameworks, CI/CD and enterprise automation.

3. ThreadLocal — GOOD

We clarified an important concept today.

Driver
ThreadLocal<WebDriver>

because:

Thread 1 → Browser 1
Thread 2 → Browser 2
Thread 3 → Browser 3
Reporting
ThreadLocal<ExtentTest>

NOT:

ThreadLocal<ExtentReports>

Because:

ExtentReports
     │
     ├── ExtentTest → Thread 1
     ├── ExtentTest → Thread 2
     └── ExtentTest → Thread 3

This is exactly the sort of question a customer interviewer may ask:

"How did you make your framework parallel-execution safe?"

You can answer:

"The WebDriver instance is maintained using ThreadLocal so every parallel test gets an isolated browser session. Similarly, ExtentTest is maintained using ThreadLocal so test logs and results remain associated with the correct executing thread. The ExtentReports instance itself remains shared."

That's a strong answer.

4. Configuration management — GOOD

We created:

execution.mode=LOCAL
browser=CHROME
headless=false
application.url=...

and:

ConfigManager.get("browser");

We also made system properties override properties-file values.

That gives you:

mvn test -Dbrowser=firefox

without changing the file.

That's important for CI/CD.

You can explain:

"Configuration is externalized so the same framework can execute across environments and browsers without modifying the source code."

Your interview-ready test strategy also explicitly calls for environment readiness and dedicated SIT/UAT/performance environments.

5. BaseTest / BasePage — GOOD

We have separated:

BaseTest

Responsible for:

Driver setup
Application navigation
Reporting initialization
Video initialization
Cleanup
BasePage

Responsible for:

protected final WebDriver driver;
protected final WaitUtils wait;

This is good separation of concerns.

6. Page Object Model — GOOD

We created:

LoginPage
HomePage

with methods such as:

enterEmail()
enterPassword()
clickLogin()
submitLogin()
logout()
isUserLoggedIn()

And we fixed an important locator issue:

❌

By.cssSelector("//div[@class='validation-summary-errors']/ul/li")

because that is XPath syntax being passed to CSS.

Correct:

By.xpath("//div[@class='validation-summary-errors']/ul/li")

or:

By.cssSelector("div.validation-summary-errors ul li")

That's a useful thing to remember for the interview.

7. AuthenticationService — GOOD DESIGN

Instead of doing this repeatedly:

new LoginPage()
    .enterEmail(...)
    .enterPassword(...)
    .clickLogin();

inside every test, we introduced:

AuthenticationService

So:

authentication.login();

can encapsulate authentication.

That's a good business-flow abstraction.

You can tell the customer:

"Page objects expose UI operations, while service-layer classes encapsulate reusable business workflows."

That's significantly better than presenting everything as simple Page Object Model.

8. Test coverage — BASIC but good starting point

