Logout

That's enough to demonstrate the framework mechanics.

But this is where I would NOT stop.

Four login tests are not an enterprise test suite.

We need to demonstrate that you know how to scale from:

4 tests

to:

500 / 1,000+ tests

Your actual experience is much stronger here: your resume says you have automated 1,000+ Salesforce regression scenarios and previously automated 2,000+ telecom scenarios.

So the demo framework should be presented as:

"A simplified reference implementation of the architecture I use for enterprise automation."

Not:

"This is my complete enterprise framework."

That distinction matters.

9. ExtentReports — GOOD, but one change recommended

We have:

reports/
    ExtentReport.html

and:

ExtentReports
    ↓
ExtentTest

with:

ThreadLocal<ExtentTest>

Good.

But there is one architectural improvement I want us to make:

Don't flush after every test in a parallel suite.

Currently BaseTest does:

@AfterMethod

and flushes.

For a genuinely parallel execution framework, I would prefer:

@BeforeSuite
    ↓
Initialize ExtentReports

@BeforeMethod
    ↓
Create ExtentTest

@Test
    ↓
Log

@AfterMethod
    ↓
Cleanup ThreadLocal<ExtentTest>

@AfterSuite
    ↓
ExtentReports.flush()

That is more appropriate for an enterprise parallel framework.

10. Video recording — GOOD differentiator

We also have:

VideoRecorderUtil.start(...)
VideoRecorderUtil.stop()

That's useful because you're demonstrating failure diagnostics beyond screenshots.

But we need to make sure you can answer:

"Do you record every test?"

A good answer:

"Not necessarily. Video recording can be enabled conditionally because recording every test increases execution overhead and storage consumption. I would typically enable it for failures, debugging runs, or selected suites."

That's a much more senior answer than simply saying "we record videos."

